1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,587
You push the course's own code,
untouched, to a repository of your own.

2
00:00:04,907 --> 00:00:08,149
Soon after, one log says:
unit tests, success.

3
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:10,389
Integration tests, success.

4
00:00:10,709 --> 00:00:12,352
System tests, success.

5
00:00:12,672 --> 00:00:13,973
All tests passed.

6
00:00:15,017 --> 00:00:18,397
And the same file,
run end to end, ends like this:

7
00:00:18,397 --> 00:00:20,628
job Lint and Format Check failed.

8
00:00:20,948 --> 00:00:24,297
Five machines green, one red,
and the whole run red.

9
00:00:24,617 --> 00:00:28,372
The pipeline says every test passed,
and the pipeline failed.

10
00:00:28,692 --> 00:00:29,822
Both are true.

11
00:00:30,067 --> 00:00:33,374
So which machine said no,
and why didn't the summary hear it?

12
00:00:33,694 --> 00:00:36,958
By the end of part 1 you will see this
on your own push.

13
00:00:37,278 --> 00:00:39,453
In part 2 we open every log.

14
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,561
In lecture 9 these checks ran
on your laptop, before a commit.

15
00:00:43,881 --> 00:00:48,081
Today they move to GitHub,
and they run on every push to main,

16
00:00:48,081 --> 00:00:50,452
on fresh machines that nobody owns.

17
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,025
Lecture 21 has four sections:
remote Git hooks,

18
00:00:54,025 --> 00:00:58,764
GitHub webhooks, continuous integration,
and continuous deployment.

19
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:03,500
We follow it slide by slide,
and the README Part 5 step by step.

20
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:08,181
To follow along, you need
a fresh clone of the course's repository,

21
00:01:08,181 --> 00:01:12,347
Docker running, a GitHub account,
and a repository of your own.

22
00:01:12,667 --> 00:01:16,977
No cloud and no keys,
and on a public repository, no bill.

23
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:22,039
The tracker names the README step,
code panels name their file and lines,

24
00:01:22,039 --> 00:01:25,473
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

25
00:01:25,793 --> 00:01:29,324
The runs you will see ran
right here on fresh machines;

26
00:01:29,324 --> 00:01:32,342
the GitHub pages are drawings,
and they say so.

27
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:36,619
The lecture opens with a recap:
local Git hooks.

28
00:01:36,939 --> 00:01:41,163
A pre-commit hook runs on your laptop,
before the commit even exists.

29
00:01:41,483 --> 00:01:44,256
A pre-push hook runs
before your commits leave.

30
00:01:44,576 --> 00:01:47,435
In lecture 9 you installed this repo's set.

31
00:01:47,683 --> 00:01:51,181
Here is that chain, with this repo's boxes:

32
00:01:51,181 --> 00:01:52,915
seven hygiene checks,

33
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:56,631
then Black, then
Flake8, both at 120 columns,

34
00:01:56,631 --> 00:01:58,990
and only on files under src/.

35
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:01,336
The lecture's chain ends with tests.

36
00:02:01,656 --> 00:02:05,198
This repo's hooks run none:
it leaves the tests to CI.

37
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:06,653
The lecture's catch:

38
00:02:06,653 --> 00:02:10,617
hooks run only on a machine
where someone ran pre-commit install.

39
00:02:10,937 --> 00:02:14,777
A teammate's laptop without them
sends its commit through unchecked.

40
00:02:15,097 --> 00:02:17,102
So the checks depend on the laptop.

41
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:19,182
That's where remote hooks come in:

42
00:02:19,182 --> 00:02:21,659
they run on the server
that receives the push.

43
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:25,231
A pre-receive hook runs
before the push is accepted,

44
00:02:25,231 --> 00:02:26,481
so it can refuse it.

45
00:02:26,801 --> 00:02:29,979
The update hook does the same,
one branch at a time.

46
00:02:30,216 --> 00:02:33,344
A post-receive hook runs
after the code is in,

47
00:02:33,344 --> 00:02:36,744
for integration tests,
notifications and clean-up.

48
00:02:37,064 --> 00:02:39,774
It can report, but it can no longer say no.

49
00:02:40,016 --> 00:02:44,688
One honest note: GitHub.com
doesn't run your own pre-receive scripts.

50
00:02:45,008 --> 00:02:47,082
It gives you two pieces instead:

51
00:02:47,082 --> 00:02:52,006
Actions, which runs after the push,
and branch protection, which can say no.

52
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:54,341
And hooks act only inside Git.

53
00:02:54,661 --> 00:02:58,223
To tell a chat what happened,
the lecture reaches for webhooks.

54
00:02:58,466 --> 00:03:01,453
Now the lecture's motivation,
in three slides.

55
00:03:01,773 --> 00:03:04,695
On your laptop,
the checks fire on every commit.

56
00:03:05,015 --> 00:03:09,432
Then you push, and on the shared repository,
nothing runs them at all.

57
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:11,674
So the checks move to the server:

58
00:03:11,674 --> 00:03:15,021
build a container, check the code,
summarize the tests.

59
00:03:15,341 --> 00:03:19,501
Same idea, new home,
for every push to main from every laptop.

60
00:03:19,821 --> 00:03:22,146
That move is continuous integration.

61
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:28,598
The lecture defines continuous integration
as a series of scripts

62
00:03:28,598 --> 00:03:31,826
that run automatically
whenever the code changes.

63
00:03:32,146 --> 00:03:34,699
They integrate changes continuously,

64
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:37,890
test automatically,
enforce coding standards,

65
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:39,592
and run static analysis.

66
00:03:39,834 --> 00:03:41,239
In ordinary software,

67
00:03:41,239 --> 00:03:45,253
that buys faster releases, better code,
and easier teamwork.

68
00:03:45,573 --> 00:03:49,156
Machine learning adds a twist;
we come back to it at the end.

69
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:51,725
Slide 21 draws it as a loop.

70
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:56,756
Developers commit to the codebase;
the codebase is built, then tested;

71
00:03:56,756 --> 00:03:59,853
and notifications flow back
to the developers.

72
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:02,670
For now, the way back is a question mark.

73
00:04:02,917 --> 00:04:05,285
The course's pipeline has five stages.

74
00:04:05,605 --> 00:04:10,636
A commit triggers it, the container
is built, Black and Flake8 check the code,

75
00:04:10,636 --> 00:04:13,861
the tests run,
and a summary reports pass or fail.

76
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:18,111
Now the README, Part 5:
GitHub Actions, remote CI.

77
00:04:18,431 --> 00:04:24,153
It says all CI tests run inside
Docker containers using Python 3.11,

78
00:04:24,153 --> 00:04:26,089
the same as production.

79
00:04:26,409 --> 00:04:31,380
This repo has no production yet:
take it as the same image you would deploy.

80
00:04:31,617 --> 00:04:35,777
This one isn't in the README, it's extra:
list the workflows folder.

81
00:04:36,097 --> 00:04:41,942
One file, ci.yml, in .github/workflows,
at the root of the repository.

82
00:04:42,262 --> 00:04:46,316
GitHub looks only there;
a workflow in a subfolder never runs.

83
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:47,550
Open it.

84
00:04:47,787 --> 00:04:51,009
The file gives the workflow a name,
CI Pipeline.

85
00:04:51,329 --> 00:04:54,209
Next comes the key called on,
just the word on.

86
00:04:54,529 --> 00:04:56,875
It lists the events that start the workflow.

87
00:04:57,195 --> 00:05:03,062
Under jobs, each job gets its own machine,
called a runner: runs-on: ubuntu-latest.

88
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,732
Inside a job, steps run in order.

89
00:05:06,052 --> 00:05:11,441
A step is either a shell script, under run,
or a reusable action, under uses,

90
00:05:11,441 --> 00:05:13,004
like actions/checkout,

91
00:05:13,004 --> 00:05:16,271
which copies your repository
onto the machine.

92
00:05:16,591 --> 00:05:20,154
Event, workflow, jobs, steps:
that is the whole grammar.

93
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:23,092
When does the pipeline wake up?

94
00:05:23,412 --> 00:05:26,377
The slide says
on every push or pull request.

95
00:05:26,697 --> 00:05:30,294
The file is more precise:
pushes to main or develop,

96
00:05:30,294 --> 00:05:32,991
and pull requests into main or develop.

97
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:35,452
Push to main, and a runner wakes up.

98
00:05:35,772 --> 00:05:37,372
Push to develop, the same.

99
00:05:37,692 --> 00:05:40,363
Push to a feature branch,
and nothing happens:

100
00:05:40,363 --> 00:05:43,964
on any other branch,
the pipeline waits for your pull request.

101
00:05:44,284 --> 00:05:48,380
A small note: the comment
at the top of the file says main only;

102
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,556
the triggers say main and develop.

103
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,800
Quick test.

104
00:05:52,101 --> 00:05:54,427
You push three commits to feature/login.

105
00:05:54,747 --> 00:05:56,069
How many runs start?

106
00:05:56,389 --> 00:05:58,715
Then you open a pull request into main.

107
00:05:59,035 --> 00:06:00,037
How many now?

108
00:06:08,883 --> 00:06:11,656
None for the three pushes:
the branch filter.

109
00:06:11,976 --> 00:06:13,873
One when the pull request opens,

110
00:06:13,873 --> 00:06:17,075
and one more for every later push
while it stays open.

111
00:06:17,317 --> 00:06:19,173
And it doesn't test your branch tip.

112
00:06:19,493 --> 00:06:23,135
GitHub makes a temporary merge
of your branch into main,

113
00:06:23,135 --> 00:06:26,256
and the commit ID the jobs see names
that merge.

114
00:06:26,576 --> 00:06:31,567
What will land is what gets tested:
each merge is tested, as the lecture says.

115
00:06:31,817 --> 00:06:35,145
The lecture warns
that integrating often costs compute.

116
00:06:35,465 --> 00:06:38,056
This file answers with a branch filter,

117
00:06:38,056 --> 00:06:41,844
one build shared by every job,
and an image kept one day.

118
00:06:42,164 --> 00:06:46,160
On private repositories every run
counts against your plan;

119
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:47,582
public ones run free.

120
00:06:47,817 --> 00:06:49,073
And timing matters:

121
00:06:49,073 --> 00:06:52,446
the run starts after GitHub
has accepted your push.

122
00:06:52,766 --> 00:06:56,435
It's the bell, not the gate:
a red run doesn't undo the push.

123
00:06:57,583 --> 00:06:58,853
Next, the README:

124
00:06:58,853 --> 00:07:02,810
6 jobs that run in parallel,
except for dependencies.

125
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:04,969
Each has an ID and a name:

126
00:07:04,969 --> 00:07:11,122
Build Docker Image, Lint and Format Check,
Unit Tests, Integration Tests, System Tests,

127
00:07:11,122 --> 00:07:12,324
and Test Summary.

128
00:07:12,566 --> 00:07:15,054
Remember the names: GitHub shows them,

129
00:07:15,054 --> 00:07:18,262
not the IDs,
and we'll need them to protect main.

130
00:07:18,500 --> 00:07:20,548
Start from the lecture's row of five.

131
00:07:20,868 --> 00:07:24,068
Commit drops out first:
it's the event, not a job.

132
00:07:24,388 --> 00:07:27,758
Build Container stays on the left:
it's the build job.

133
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,389
Now each needs line draws one arrow.

134
00:07:30,709 --> 00:07:32,608
Lint and format needs build.

135
00:07:32,928 --> 00:07:37,451
Unit tests, integration tests
and system tests each need build too.

136
00:07:37,771 --> 00:07:41,033
So after the build,
four jobs run side by side,

137
00:07:41,033 --> 00:07:42,698
each on its own machine.

138
00:07:42,933 --> 00:07:45,557
Test summary needs the three test jobs.

139
00:07:45,877 --> 00:07:48,842
Look closely:
the lint job isn't on that list.

140
00:07:49,162 --> 00:07:51,727
The lecture lists the stages in order;

141
00:07:51,727 --> 00:07:57,397
on GitHub, four of them run side by side,
and the summary listens only to the tests.

142
00:07:57,717 --> 00:07:59,210
Keep that gap in mind.

143
00:07:59,450 --> 00:08:02,924
An extra check, not in the README:
a tool called act,

144
00:08:02,924 --> 00:08:07,578
which you don't need, takes the same file
and lists the jobs in stages.

145
00:08:07,898 --> 00:08:09,349
Stage 0, build.

146
00:08:09,669 --> 00:08:11,503
Stage 1, the four checks.

147
00:08:11,823 --> 00:08:13,338
Stage 2, the summary.

148
00:08:13,583 --> 00:08:14,991
Then the key concepts.

149
00:08:15,311 --> 00:08:19,574
Artifact sharing and unique image tags come
with the build job;

150
00:08:19,574 --> 00:08:21,807
parallel execution, you just saw;

151
00:08:21,807 --> 00:08:26,340
conditional execution and network modes come
with the system tests.

152
00:08:26,583 --> 00:08:29,239
The README's screenshot gives the times:

153
00:08:29,239 --> 00:08:33,090
about 2 minutes on the clock,
with four jobs side by side.

154
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:38,147
On a private repository, each job is billed
and rounded up separately:

155
00:08:38,147 --> 00:08:39,703
about 6 billed minutes.

156
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:41,938
Time to push.

157
00:08:42,258 --> 00:08:45,432
Start from a fresh clone,
not your lecture 9 folder:

158
00:08:45,432 --> 00:08:50,194
that one has the Black fix committed,
and your first run would come out green.

159
00:08:50,514 --> 00:08:54,695
Clone, change into the folder,
and git status says the tree is clean.

160
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,435
Your prompt shows your own user.

161
00:09:00,755 --> 00:09:02,243
Two extra checks first:

162
00:09:02,243 --> 00:09:06,838
the pinned commit is still the head of main
on the course's repository,

163
00:09:06,838 --> 00:09:10,333
and the last two commits
are both called demo class 1.

164
00:09:10,653 --> 00:09:14,216
The second undid a bug planted in class;
we'll run it later.

165
00:09:14,450 --> 00:09:16,775
Now the README block, Push to GitHub.

166
00:09:17,095 --> 00:09:18,909
git add . — no output.

167
00:09:19,229 --> 00:09:22,365
Then git commit -m "Add CI pipeline".

168
00:09:22,685 --> 00:09:26,141
And Git answers:
nothing to commit, working tree clean.

169
00:09:26,383 --> 00:09:29,863
A small slip, and a harmless one:
in a clone or a fork,

170
00:09:29,863 --> 00:09:33,786
the pipeline is already committed,
so there is nothing to add.

171
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:38,342
Before the README's git push,
an extra check: git remote -v.

172
00:09:38,662 --> 00:09:42,844
Origin is still the course's repository,
and you can't write to it.

173
00:09:43,164 --> 00:09:46,449
GitHub would refuse a push from here:
permission denied.

174
00:09:46,683 --> 00:09:49,584
So point origin at a repository you own.

175
00:09:49,904 --> 00:09:55,378
Either fork the course's repository
and enable workflows on its Actions tab,

176
00:09:55,378 --> 00:09:57,755
or create a new empty repository.

177
00:09:58,075 --> 00:10:02,576
Either way, run git remote set-url origin,
with your own address.

178
00:10:02,896 --> 00:10:04,475
Pause and set yours up.

179
00:10:07,683 --> 00:10:10,534
This recording machine can't push to GitHub,

180
00:10:10,534 --> 00:10:14,616
so an empty repository
on the same machine stands in for yours.

181
00:10:14,936 --> 00:10:17,710
From now on we just say your repository.

182
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:19,550
Now the README's git push.

183
00:10:19,870 --> 00:10:21,534
New branch, main -> main.

184
00:10:21,854 --> 00:10:24,734
This push is the event,
and run 1 starts now.

185
00:10:25,054 --> 00:10:26,483
Pause and push yours.

186
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:30,033
On a fork, that commit is already on GitHub,

187
00:10:30,033 --> 00:10:33,636
so git push says Everything up-to-date
and starts nothing.

188
00:10:33,956 --> 00:10:37,138
To get run 1 anyway, push an empty commit:

189
00:10:37,138 --> 00:10:41,380
git commit --allow-empty -m "Trigger CI",
then git push.

190
00:10:41,700 --> 00:10:45,519
Or wait, and your first run
then comes with the fix in part 2.

191
00:10:46,867 --> 00:10:51,710
Watch GitHub Actions: go to your repository,
and click the Actions tab.

192
00:10:52,030 --> 00:10:56,766
Your run is listed under the commit message,
with a yellow dot: in progress.

193
00:10:57,086 --> 00:11:00,819
A green checkmark means success;
a red X means failure.

194
00:11:01,139 --> 00:11:02,696
Pause and open yours.

195
00:11:02,934 --> 00:11:07,287
Click it, and GitHub draws the run
as the graph you just built:

196
00:11:07,287 --> 00:11:11,019
build first, four jobs side by side,
the summary last.

197
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:12,867
A word on how we show it.

198
00:11:13,187 --> 00:11:15,885
We ran every job of this file right here,

199
00:11:15,885 --> 00:11:20,163
each on a brand-new machine
with an empty Docker, as GitHub does.

200
00:11:20,483 --> 00:11:25,006
And a tool called act ran the whole file
a second way, with the same verdict.

201
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:29,497
So the GitHub pages you see
are drawings built from the workflow file,

202
00:11:29,497 --> 00:11:30,711
marked illustration.

203
00:11:31,031 --> 00:11:32,973
Every log line in them is real.

204
00:11:33,217 --> 00:11:35,457
A question, before the colors come in.

205
00:11:35,777 --> 00:11:37,782
You already know this run turns red.

206
00:11:38,102 --> 00:11:42,881
Is your push on main in your repository now,
or did the red run refuse it?

207
00:11:51,717 --> 00:11:52,998
Yes, it's on main:

208
00:11:52,998 --> 00:11:57,840
the push landed before any job started,
and a red run can't undo it.

209
00:11:58,160 --> 00:11:59,461
Build: green.

210
00:11:59,781 --> 00:12:01,872
Lint and Format Check: red.

211
00:12:02,192 --> 00:12:04,944
The three tests and Test Summary: green.

212
00:12:05,264 --> 00:12:06,586
The run: red.

213
00:12:06,834 --> 00:12:11,293
Those checks and crosses next to your commit
are the lecture's notifications.

214
00:12:11,613 --> 00:12:16,284
And act, the second witness, agrees:
five jobs succeeded, one failed.

215
00:12:16,534 --> 00:12:19,734
A kind note:
the README's screenshot is all green.

216
00:12:20,054 --> 00:12:22,337
It shows an earlier version of the code.

217
00:12:22,657 --> 00:12:25,984
Your first run is red,
and in part 2 you turn it green.

218
00:12:26,234 --> 00:12:31,034
And here is the hook, on your own push:
two of these six machines disagree.

219
00:12:31,354 --> 00:12:34,852
In part 2 we open every log,
one job at a time.

